# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the concurrent load test (scripts/sanitizer_load_test.sh tsan).
#
# mimalloc 3.x manages its per-thread heap and internal metadata with lock-free
# atomics whose happens-before edges ThreadSanitizer does not fully model, so it
# reports benign races inside the allocator's own thread-heap/meta bookkeeping
# when many worker threads allocate concurrently under load. mimalloc is
# thread-safe; these are false positives in the third-party allocator's
# internals, not defects in Ruvia code.
#
# The tsanAllocatorAcquire/Release annotations in ruvia-core/src/MemoryPool.cpp
# already teach TSan about the mi_malloc/mi_free BLOCK reuse path; that path is
# NOT suppressed here. This file covers only mimalloc's internal META/thread-heap
# path, which those annotations cannot reach.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow -- only mimalloc's own internal frames (the
# underscore-prefixed internals Ruvia never calls directly). A genuine Ruvia
# data race surfaces in ruvia:: frames and is never hidden by these entries.
# The proper long-term fix is to build mimalloc with MI_TSAN=1; until the vcpkg
# port exposes that, these suppressions keep the load test usable.
race:_mi_theap_init
race:_mi_meta_alloc
race:_mi_meta_free
